            <section class="mt-content-container">
                <div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="Valence_shell_electron_pair_repulsion_(VSEPR)_theory"></span><h2 class="editable">Valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR) theory</h2>

<p>Lewis structures show bonds and lone pairs around atoms in a molecule, but they do not predict the angles between them. For example, Lewis structure of water may be writteh as \(\ce{H-\overset{\!\Large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{\underset{\underset{\huge{H}} |}{O}}\!:}\) or \(\ce{H-\overset{\!\Large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{\underset{\!\Large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{O}}-H}\) both shoing two bonds and two lone pairs around \(\ce{O}\) which correct, the firt implies 180<sup>o</sup> angle betwen the bonds and second 90<sup>o</sup>, which are incorrect. The experimentally measured bond angle in water is 104.5&deg;.</p>

<section class="box-definition">
<ul>
    <li><strong>Valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR) </strong>predicts the geometry of atoms around a central atom in a molecule, based on repulsion between electron groups on the central atom.</li>
    <li>The <strong>central atom</strong> is an atom bonded to more than one atom in the molecule, e.g., \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{H-\overset{\!\Large\:\cdot\:\!\cdot}{\underset{\underset{\huge{H}} |}{O}}\!:}\)</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Electron_groups"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Electron groups</span></h5>

<p>The electron groups are assigned to an atom in a molecule as follows.</p>

<ol>
    <li>an unpaired  electron as on \(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{ :\!\overset{\color{red}\Large\cdot}{N}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!: }\) is one electron group,</li>
    <li>two electrons of a lone pair, as on \(\ce{N}\) in  \(\ce{:\!\overset{\Large\cdot}{N}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!: }\) is one electron group (note: two lone piars on \(\ce{O}\) are two separate electrons groups),</li>
    <li>two bonded electrons in a single bond as on \(\ce{C}\) in  \(\ce{H-C\!\equiv\!{N}\!:}\) is one electron group,</li>
    <li>four electrons in a double bond as on \(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{ :\!\overset{\Large\cdot}{N}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!: }\) is one electron group, and </li>
    <li>six electrons in a triple bond as on \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{H-C\!\equiv\!{N}\!:}\) is one electron group.</li>
</ol>

<p>\(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{:\!\overset{\color{red}\Large\cdot}{N}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!: }\) has a total of three electron groups, an unpaired electron, a lone pair, and a double bond. \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{:\!\overset{\color{red}\Large\cdot}{N}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!: }\) also has a total of three electron groups, two lone pairs and a double bond. \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{H-C\!\equiv\!{N}\!:}\) has two electron groups, a single bond, and a triple bond.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Electron_group_geometry_and_molecular_geometry"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Electron group geometry and molecular geometry</span></h5>

<p>The geometry of electron groups surrounding the central atom in a molecule is referred to as <strong>electron group geometry</strong>. The electron groups may be bonding or nonbonding electrons. If electron groups are all bonding electron pairs, the electron group geometry is the same as the molecule geometry. However, if some electron groups are nonbonding, e.g., lone pairs, the geometry of atoms connected by bonds is called <strong>molecular geometry</strong>.</p>


</div></section>

<p>Diatomic molecules like \(\ce{H-H}\), \(\ce{H-Cl}\), and \(\ce{Cl-Cl}\) are always linear. The geometry of the molecule containing three or more atoms is determined by the number of electron groups around the central atom, as explained below.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Two_electron_groups"></span><h3 class="editable">Two electron groups</h3>

<p><img alt="Linear structure of H-Be-H" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486483/clipboard_e38e0b652682a6c6509ecd2a3fe3933f0.png?revision=1" />Two electron groups are farthest apart at 180&deg; in a linear arrangement. Therefore, the electron group geometry and the molecular geometry are linear if the central atom has two electron groups, as:  <img style="width: 73.9962px; height: 21.9981px;" width="73.9962px" height="21.9981px" class="internal" alt="AX2E0-3D-balls.png" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/493572/AX2E0-3D-balls.png?revision=1" />. For example, \(\ce{Be}\) in \(\ce{H-Be-H}\) and \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{H-C\!\equiv\!{N}\!:}\) and  \(\ce{:\!{\underset{\Large{\cdot\cdot}}{O}}=C={\underset{\Large{\cdot\cdot}}{O}}\!:}\) have two electron groups around the central \(\ce{C}\) atom, resulting in a linear geometry of these molecules, as illustrated in the figures on the right. Examples of organic compounds are those that have a triple bond on \(\ce{C}\), e.g., \(\ce{H-C\equiv C-H}\). Note that this compound has two \(\ce{C}\) atoms, both of which are central atoms with linear geometry, resulting in four atoms in a row on the line.</p>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Hydrocarbons"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Hydrocarbons</span></h5>

<p>Organic compounds containing only \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{H}\) are called <strong>hydrocarbons</strong>.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Alkynes"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Alkynes</span></h5>

<p> Hydrocarbons having at least one \(\ce{C\equiv C}\) bond are called <strong>alkyne</strong>.</p>
</div></section>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="Three_electron_groups"></span><h3 class="editable">Three electron groups</h3>

<p><img alt="Triangular planar structure of BF3" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486485/clipboard_eafda8f9637d6265e85d7f65e52b54835.png?revision=1" /><img alt="Triagular planar structure of SO3" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486486/clipboard_e2328db90a5eb80a64a7e9efb04a0c253.png?revision=1" />Three electron groups are farthest apart at 120&deg; in a plane, as <img alt="clipboard_e5dfa62c05c1bec87fd6304f1fa39e501.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486702/clipboard_e5dfa62c05c1bec87fd6304f1fa39e501.png?revision=1" />. For example, geometry around \(\ce{B}\) in \(\ce{BF3}\) and around \(\ce{S}\) in \(\ce{SO3}\), as illustrated in the figure on the right and left. Lines drawn between peripheral atoms make a triangle. Therefore, this geometry is referred to as triangular planar geometry. Examples of carbons in organic compound having triangular planar geometry are those that have a double bond on them, e.g., \(\ce{H2C=CH2}\) and \(\ce{H2C=O}\), as illustrated in the figure on the right.</p>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Alkene"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Alkene</span></h5>

<p>Hydrocarbons having at least one \(\ce{C=C} \) are called <strong>alkenes</strong>.<img alt="Triangular planar geometry of H2C=O" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486699/clipboard_e37b3da24df08fbc72b0df6d8e29b8347.png?revision=1" /><img alt="Trangular planar geometry of H2C=CH2" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486698/clipboard_e181e71b248a2265aeab21881035ef7b7.png?revision=1" /></p>
</div></section>

<p>If one of the three electron groups is a lone pair, the electron group geometry is still triangular planar. Still, the molecule geometry, i.e., the geometry of atoms in the molecule around the central atom, is bent. For example, geometry around \(\ce{S}\) in \(\ce{:\!{\underset{\Large{\cdot\cdot}}{O}}=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{S}={\underset{\Large{\cdot\cdot}}{O}}\!:}\) is bent, as shown in the figure on the left.<img alt="Bent geometry of SO2" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486489/clipboard_e45c7ef73ba4c2f4994fc57a1b22f2d94.png?revision=1" />   </p>

<p><img alt="Triangular planar structure of BF3" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486491/clipboard_ee45545feae4fcf0986f52a57b52fbe31.png?revision=1" />Another example of a triangular planar geometry is the geometry around \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{Cl2C=O}\), as shown in the figure on the right. Note that the bond angle between \(\ce{Cl-C-Cl}\) is 111.8&deg;, i.e., less than the ideal value 120&deg;. This is because repulsion between the double bond and a single bond is stronger than between a single bond and a single bond.</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Order_of_repulsion_force_between_electron_groups"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Order of repulsion force between electron groups</span></h5>

<p>Repulsion force between different types of electron groups usually increases in the following order: single bond to single bond &lt; single bond to double bond &lt; single bond to lone pair &lt; lone pair to lone pair.</p>
</div></section>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_4"><span id="Four_electron_groups"></span><h3 class="editable">Four electron groups</h3>

<p><img alt="A regular tetrahedron within a cube" class="left internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/493571/Tetraeder_animation_with_cube.gif?revision=1" /><img alt="Tetrahederal CH4 within a cube" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486705/clipboard_ea0dfb1e8b34bd46d92e5bf6e6fcedccc.png?revision=1" />Four electron groups are farthest apart at 109.5&deg; from each other in a 3D geometry called tetrahedral geometry <img alt="clipboard_ec319e6ecfff70b864bdcd58037514430.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486703/clipboard_ec319e6ecfff70b864bdcd58037514430.png?revision=1" />. To understand this geometry, consider a cube, cut off alternate corners, and join the remaining four corners with each other by lines, it results in a tetrahedral geometry, as illustrated by yellow polyhedron within a cube in the figure on the left (Copyright: Andr&eacute; Karwath aka Aka, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons). It is a regular tetrahedron having four equilateral triangular faces. Now consider a cube with \(\ce{C}\) in its center and four \(\ce{C-H}\) bonds pointing to alternate corners of the cube, as illustrated in the figure on the right (copyright: Public domain). This is a tetrahedral geometry of \(\ce{CH4}\) molecule having four electron groups (single bonds) around the central \(\ce{C}\) with a 109.5&deg; angle between all bonds. </p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Perspective_drawing_of_a_tetrahedral_.5C(.5Cce.7BC.7D.5C)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Perspective drawing of a tetrahedral \(\ce{C}\)</span></h5>

<p>Note that the tetrahedral \(\ce{CH4}\) inserted in a cube, as shown in the figure on the right, is composed of two Vs of 109.5&deg; internal angle joined at vortices and oriented perpendicular to each other. One V of \(\ce{H-C-H}\) bonds in the plane of the page is shown by solid lines in red. The other V of \(\ce{H-C-H}\) bonds shown in blue and viewed slightly from the top-right corner, has one \(\ce{C-H}\) bond protruding out of the page towards the viewer is represented by a solid wedge, and the other \(\ce{C-H}\) bond receding to the back of the page away from the viewer is represented by a hashed wedge. The red V in the plane of the page appears wider than the blue V perpendicular to the page. This is a conventional way of representing the perspective drawing of a 3D tetrahedral molecular geometry. The wider V in the plane of the page may be shown pointing up, down, left, or right, as illustrated in Figure 1.4.1 and the interactive model of of \(\ce{CH4}\) molecule is presented in Figure 1.4.2.</p>
</div></section>

