<p><img alt="trigonal pyrimidal geometry of NH3" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486716/clipboard_e18d25e541ecc9a11a902b5cee25eb7cd.png?revision=1" />If one of the four electron groups is a lone pair, the electron group geometry is tetrahedral, but the molecule geometry is trigonal pyramidal, as <img alt="clipboard_e0cd674c1e236d548961eb6275b4e5895.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486714/clipboard_e0cd674c1e236d548961eb6275b4e5895.png?revision=1" />. The name is based on the fact that connecting the three peripheral atoms makes a triangle, and the central atoms are raised in the middle, creating a trigonal pyramidal shape. For example, \(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{H3N\!:}\) has a trigonal pyramidal shape, as shown in the figure on the right. Note that \(\ce{H-N-H}\) angle has been reduced from the perfect value of 109.5&deg; to 107.8&deg; because lone pair to bond pair repulsion is stronger than bond pair to bond pair repulsion.  </p>

<p><img alt="bent geometry of H2O" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486718/clipboard_e0527ccc30cf676c6a5ab2eefc87f48e0.png?revision=1" />If two of the four electron groups are lone pairs, the electron group geometry is tetrahedral, but the molecule geometry is bent, as <img alt="clipboard_e0a0ad947fce49589e9a5bce68dbf11cd.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486717/clipboard_e0a0ad947fce49589e9a5bce68dbf11cd.png?revision=1" />. For example, \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{H2\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!:}\) is bent, as shown in the figure on the left. Note hat \(\ce{H-O-H}\) angle has been reduced from the perfect value of 109.5&deg; to 104.5&deg; because lone pair to lone pair repulsion is stronger than bond pair to bond pair repulsion.</p>
</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_5"><span id="Valence_bond_theory"></span><h2 class="editable">Valence bond theory</h2>

<p>Lewis model of bonding predicts the number of bonds and the bond type: single, double, or triple, around an atom in a molecule. VSEPR theory predicts the bond angles and geometry around the central atom in a molecule. These two models are empirical and offer limited explanation. Still, they are used because they are simple. Two models, valence bond (VB) theory and molecular orbital theory (MO) theory, have been developed that take into account the orbitals of electrons. These are more complex, but not only predict the bonds and geometries, they can also predict additional properties, including bond strength, spectroscopic properties, and magnetic properties of the molecules. Among VB and MO theories, VB theory is relatively simple and more often used in organic chemistry. Although MO theory is more complex, it is still used because some of the chemical properties can be modeled better or exclusively by it. Here, a VB theory will be described, followed by a brief introduction to MO theory. Later in this course, a hybrid approach of VB and MO theories will be used to explain the chemical properties of organic molecules.</p>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p>According to the valence bond theory, as atoms approach each other, their singly occupied valence orbitals overlap, resulting in the pairing of the two electrons in the overlapped orbital. Forces of attraction between both the nuclei and the two electrons in the overlapped orbitals primarily hold the two nuclei together, forming a covalent bond. </p>
</section>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_6"><span id="Mechanism_of_covalent_bond_formation"></span><h3 class="editable">Mechanism of covalent bond formation</h3>

<p class="lt-chem-394085">Atoms in the liquid or gas state move around due to thermal energy. Atoms have both positive-charge nuclei and negative-charge electrons. When two atoms approach each other, attractive forces between opposite charges and repulsive forces between the same charges develop. The two atoms move towards each other due to the attractive force, decreasing their potential energy until the minimum potential energy is reached. Decreasing the internuclear distance further from the optimum value increases repulsive forces more than attractive forces, as illustrated in Figure 1.4.4.</p>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e31b34e322439ec49f074a3a15b85137d.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487068/clipboard_e31b34e322439ec49f074a3a15b85137d.png?revision=1" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\): Illustration of potential energy vs. internuclear distance during a covalent bond formation between two \(\ce{H}\) atoms. (Copyright; Public domain)</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Bond_length.2C_bond_energy.2C_and_the_bond_strength"></span><h5 class="box-legend     lt-chem-394085 editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Bond length, bond energy, and the bond strength</span></h5>

<p class="lt-chem-394085"><strong><img alt="Average bond energies" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/494464/clipboard_e99566de00474ce6a434d67d2f0705fc0.png?revision=1" /><img alt="Average bond lengths" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/494465/clipboard_e7c6fc0f7d4fc3232f0cdd8f0213b52b5.png?revision=1" />Bond length</strong> is the distance between the centers of the two atoms at the potential energy minimum in bond formation. For example, the bond distance in \(\ce{H-H}\) is 74 pm.<strong> </strong></p>

<p class="lt-chem-394085">The difference in the energy of isolated atoms and the energy state at the bonding distance is the <strong>bond energy</strong>. For example, the bond energy of \(\ce{H-H}\) is 432 kJ/mol as shown in Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\).</p>

<p class="lt-chem-394085">Bond energy is always released when the bond is formed, and the same amount of energy is absorbed when the same bond is broken. <strong>Stronger bonds</strong> have higher bond energy, and <strong>weaker bonds</strong> have lower bond energy.</p>

<p class="lt-chem-394085">&nbsp;</p>
</div></section>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_7"><span id="Sigma_.5C(.5Csigma.5C)_and_pi_.5C(.5Cpi.5C)_bonds"></span><h3 class="editable">Sigma \(\sigma\) and pi \(\pi\) bonds</h3>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Sigma_(.5C(.5Csigma.5C))_bond"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Sigma (\(\sigma\)) bond</span></h5>

<p>A <strong>sigma \(\sigma\) bond</strong> is formed by head-on overlap of atomic orbitals such that the overlapped orbital places the electron density mostly between the two nuclei symmetrically on and around the bonding axis.</p>
</div></section>

<p>When an \(s\) orbital overlaps with an \(s\) orbital, a \(\sigma_{s-s}\) bond is formed, e.g., \(\ce{H-H}\) is a \(\sigma_{1s-1s}\) bond. An \(s\) orbital overlaps with a \(p\) orbital by approaching it along the axis of the \(p\) orbital, forming an \(\sigma_{s-p}\) bond, e.g., \(\ce{H-F}\) is a \(\sigma_{1s-2p}\) bond. When two \(p\) orbitals overlap by approaching each other along their axis, they make a \(\sigma_{p-p}\) bond, e.g., \(\ce{F-F}\) is a \(\sigma_{2p-2p}\) bond. An \(s\) orbital is spherically symmetrical and it always makes a \(\sigma\) bond. A single bond is always a \(\sigma\) bond. Figure 1.4.5 illustrates the schematics of these \(\sigma\) bonds.</p>

<figure role="group">
<div class="row">
<figure><img alt="clipboard_ed52fba1867e53ccb72fd1ee82370915d.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486815/clipboard_ed52fba1867e53ccb72fd1ee82370915d.png?revision=1" /></figure>

