<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Pi_(.5C(.5Cpi.5C))_bond"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Pi (\(\pi\)) bond</span></h5>

<p>A \(\pi\) bond is formed when a lateral overlap happens between two lobes of an orbital on one atom with two lobes of the other orbital on the second atom, overlapping, placing electron density above and below the bond axis. There is a nodal plane passing through the bond axis, which is the common nodal plane of the two overlapping orbitals.</p>
</div></section>

<p> For example, when two \(p\) orbitals overlap by approaching each other with their axes parallel, they overlap sideways, forming a \(\pi_{p-p}\) bond. The \(\pi\)-bonding is not as effective an overlap as a \(\sigma\) bond, and, consequently, a \(\pi\) bond is weaker than a \(\sigma\) bond between the same orbitals. Usually, \(\pi\) bond is the second bond in a double bond or the second and the third bond in a triple bond because only one orbital in an atom can approach head-on, forming one \(\sigma\) bond with another atom. Figure 1.4.5 illustrates the schematics of a \(\pi_{p-p}\) bond.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="Hybrid_orbitals"></span><h3 class="editable">Hybrid orbitals</h3>

<p>The valence bond theory could not explain the geometry of several molecules. For example, the electron configuration of carbon is \(\underset{1s}{\boxed{\uparrow\downarrow}}\,\underset{2s}{\boxed{\uparrow\downarrow}}\, \underset{2p}{\boxed{\;\uparrow\;}\!\boxed{\;\uparrow\;}}\). It has two unpaired electrons and should form two bonds. It does not agree with four bonds to carbon in \(\ce{CH4}\). Assuming an electron is excited and jumps form \(2s\) to \(2p\) valence orbital resulting in the electron configuration \(\underset{1s}{\boxed{\uparrow\downarrow}}\,\underset{2s}{\boxed{\uparrow}}\, \underset{2p}{\boxed{\;\uparrow\;}\!\boxed{\;\uparrow\;}\!\boxed{\;\uparrow\;}}\). Now it can make four bonds, releasing more energy than the energy consumed in the excitation of an electron. Still, it is expected to have three bonds from \(p\) orbitals at 90&deg; angles and one from \(s\) orbital at any angle. It does not agree with the tetrahedral geometry around \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{CH4}\) with all bonds equal and all bond angles equal to 109.5&deg;. This deficiency is addressed by introducing the concept of hybrid orbitals in the valence bond theory, which form bonds in molecules. </p>

<section class="box-emphasis">
<p>Orbital hybridization is a concept of mixing some valence orbitals, resulting in an equal number of hybrid orbitals, suitable for bond making, and of different energies and shapes than the constituent orbitals.</p>
</section>

<p>For example, one \(2s\) orbital of \(\ce{C}\) can mix with three \(2p\) orbitals resulting in four equal hybrid orbitals, designated as \(sp^{3}\) orbitals. Note that the superscript three does not represent electrons occupying the hybrid orbital; it tells the number of orbital types mixed to form the hybrid orbital, i.e., one \(2s\) and three \(2p \) orbitals. Similarly, one \(2s\) orbital of \(\ce{C}\) can mix with two \(2p\) orbitals resulting in three equal hybrid orbitals, designated as \(sp^{2}\) orbitals leaving one \(2p\) orbital unchanged, and one \(2s\) orbital can mix with one \(2p\) orbitals resulting in two equal hybrid orbitals, designated as \(sp\) orbitals, leaving two \(2p\) orbital unchanged in the valence shell. The shapes and geometries of these hybrid orbitals are described next.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_9"><span id=".5C(sp.5E.7B3.7D.5C)_Hybrid_orbitals"></span><h4 class="editable">\(sp^{3}\) Hybrid orbitals</h4>

<p><img alt="tetrahederal geometry of CH4 illustrated" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486996/clipboard_e01ab25bea9c2e624aed7548605153dad.png?revision=1" /><img alt="3d simulation of sp3 orbitals" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487003/clipboard_eab803cdf85e91fcb695afa9b1030b270.png?revision=1" /><img alt="tetrahedearl geometry of sp3 hybrid orbitals" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487004/clipboard_e66885fd4f1042a1a8140192559002892.png?revision=1" />An \(s\) orbital is spherically symmetrical, <img alt="clipboard_e373f66775e8ee820f36b5b7642fb840e.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486992/clipboard_e373f66775e8ee820f36b5b7642fb840e.png?revision=1" /> and a \(p\) orbital is shaped with two lobes <img alt="clipboard_ebf3857ffcff33aeed021e66b675cd7b4.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486993/clipboard_ebf3857ffcff33aeed021e66b675cd7b4.png?revision=1" />. One \(s\) and three \(p\) valence shell orbitals mix to form four equal \(sp^{3}\) orbitals with one small and one large lobes <img alt="clipboard_e3d1b5d93f48750c45959818a690032b7.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/486994/clipboard_e3d1b5d93f48750c45959818a690032b7.png?revision=1" /> that orient at 109.5&deg; from each other in tetrahedral geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the right (Copyright: Jfmelero, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>

<p><img alt="3d simulation of ethan bonding orbitals" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487012/clipboard_e381bb94d916092d2a8feeddb14ecb3da.png?revision=1" /><img alt="perspective drawing of ethane" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487013/clipboard_e065cd346335bfb1fc281f33c146efd79.png?revision=1" />Four valence electrons of \(\ce{C}\) singly occupy the four \(sp^3\) orbitals. The \(sp^3\) orbitals overlap with suitable valence orbitals of the other atoms, sharing their electron, and thus, making \(\sigma\) bonds. For example, \(sp^3\) orbitals of \(\ce{C}\) overlap \(1s\) orbital of \(\ce{H}\) resulting in \(\ce{CH4}\) molecule having four \(\sigma_{s-sp^3}\) bond in a tetrahedral geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the left and a simulation on the right (Copyrigt: Public domain). Similarly, \(\ce{C-C}\) single bond is a \(\sigma_{sp^{3}-sp^{3}}\) bond, e.g., \(\ce{H3C-CH3}\) has a \(\ce{C-C}\)\(\sigma_{sp^{3}-sp^{3}}\) bond and six \(\sigma_{s-sp^3}\) bonds with \(\ce{H}\), as illustrated in 3D-simulation and a perspective drawing on the left (Copyright: Public domain).</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_10"><span id=".5C(sp.5E.7B2.7D.5C)_Hybrid_orbitals"></span><h4 class="editable">\(sp^{2}\) Hybrid orbitals</h4>

<p><img alt="shape of thee sp2 orbitals" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487015/clipboard_ea07babec78880863fa0c988035749705.png?revision=1" /><img alt="3D simulation of sp2 orbitals of ethene." class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487016/clipboard_e363b0829e666f8eb867149038834d9d0.png?revision=1" />One \(s\) and two \(p\) valence shell orbitals mix to form three equal \(sp^2\) hybrid orbitals of similar shape to \(sp^3\), but have a larger lobe which is, on average, closer to the nucleus than \(sp^3\). The three \(sp^2\) hybrid orbitals are oriented at 120&deg; from each other in a triangular planar geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the right (Copyright: Jfmelero, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>

<p>One valence \(p\) orbital that is not hybridized remains in the valence shell oriented perpendicular to the plane of the \(sp^2\) orbitals. The four valence electrons of \(\ce{C}\) are distributed one each in the three \(sp^2\) and one \(p\) orbitals. The \(sp^2\) hybrid orbitals make \(\sigma\) bonds, and the \(p\) orbital makes a \(\pi\) bond by a side-on overlap with a \(p\) orbital of the other bonded atom. For example, \(\ce{H2C=CH2}\) has both carbons \(sp^2\) hybridized, joined with each other by a \(\sigma_{sp^{2}-sp^{2}}\) bond and a \(\pi_{p-p}\) bond. There are four \(\sigma_{s-sp^2}\) bonds with \(1s\) orbital of \(\ce{H}\), as illustrated in the 3D simulation, showing \(sp^2\) hybrid orbitals in blue and \(p\) orbital in yellow (Copyright: Public domain). Figure 1.4.6 illustrates the schematics of the \(\pi\) bond in \(\ce{H2C=CH2}\).</p>

