</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_11"><span id=".5C(sp.5C)_Hybrid_orbitals"></span><h4 class="editable">\(sp\) Hybrid orbitals</h4>

<p><img alt="Illustration of the shape and linear geometry of sp orbitals" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487020/clipboard_e10669a31b8c748586e7c17b53d81b7fb.png?revision=1" /><img alt="3D simulation of sp and 2p orbitals in sp hybrid carbons" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487021/clipboard_eca843b446a4334e686688868ee202c6b.png?revision=1" />One \(s\) and one \(p\) valence shell orbitals mix to form two equal \(sp\) hybrid orbitals of similar shape to \(sp^3\), but have a larger lobe which is, on average, closer to the nucleus than \(sp^2\) and \(sp^3\). The two \(sp\) hybrid orbitals are oriented at 180&deg; from each other in a linear geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the right (Copyright: Jfmelero, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons).</p>

<p>Two valence \(p\) orbitals that are not hybridized remain in the valence shell oriented perpendicular to \(sp^2\) orbitals and perpendicular to each other. The four valence electrons of \(\ce{C}\) are distributed one each in the tw \(sp\) and two \(p\) orbitals. The \(sp\) hybrid orbitals make \(\sigma\) bonds, and the \(p\) orbitals make two \(\pi\) bonds by a side-on overlap with \(p\) orbitals of the other bonded atoms. For example, \(\ce{HC\equiv CH}\) has both carbons \(sp\) hybridized, joined with each other by a \(\sigma_{sp-sp}\) bond and two \(\pi_{p-p}\) bonds. There are two \(\sigma_{s-sp}\) bonds with \(1s\) orbital of \(\ce{H}\), as illustrated in the 3D simulation, showing \(sp\) hybrid orbitals in blue and \(p\) orbitals in yellow (Copyright: Public domain). Figure 1.4.7 illustrates the schematics of the \(\pi\) bonds in \(\ce{HC\equiv CH}\).</p>

