</figure>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_12"><span id="Hybridization_in_atoms_other_than_.5C(.5Cce.7BC.7D.5C)"></span><h4 class="editable">Hybridization in atoms other than \(\ce{C}\)</h4>

<p><img alt="illustration of tetrahederal geometry of NH3" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487038/clipboard_e2f05ff36f6ed15f7fa3d0a28ec8fafa7.png?revision=1" /><img alt="tetrahederal geometry of [NH4]+ illustrated" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487039/clipboard_eab075736838ea3441295f1d16f02c4e7.png?revision=1" />The concept of valence orbital hybridization is not limited to \(\ce{C}\), it applies to other atoms as well. The difference is in the number of valence electrons, resulting in a different number of bonds and lone pairs. For example, \(\ce{N}\) has five valence electrons. When \(\ce{N}\) makes all single bonds, it is \(sp^3\) hybridized with three \(sp^3\) orbitals singly occupied that make three single bonds with other atoms, and the fourth \(sp^3\) orbital is filled with two electrons that remains non-bonded as a lone pair. For example, \(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{H3N\!:}\) is \(sp^3\) hybridized with three \(\sigma_{s-sp^{3}}\) bonds with \(\ce{H}\) and one lone pair occupying the fourth \(sp^3\) orbital, and trigonal pyramadial molecular geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the right. The \(\ce{H-N-H}\) angles are 107.8&deg;, a little less than ideal 109.5&deg;, due to stronger lone pair-to-bond pair repulsion than bond pair-to-bond pair repulsion. \(\ce{[NH4]^+}\) has one less valence electron and it has tetrahederal geometry with four \(\sigma_{s-sp^3}\) bond with \(\ce{H}\) and no lone pair, like \(\ce{CH4}\), as illustrated in the figure on the left. \(\ce{H3C-\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{N}H2}\) is an example of \(sp^3\) hybridized \(\ce{N}\) in organic compound. <img alt="tetrahederal geometry of PCl3 illustrated" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487041/clipboard_eededcbaeb19dda17c6ac26eb9b2979ae.png?revision=1" />\(\ce{P}\) is in the same group with \(\ce{N}\) having five valence electrons. In some compounds, like \(\ce{Cl3P\!:}\) is \(sp^3\) hybridized with three \(\sigma_{s-sp^{3}}\) bonds with \(\ce{Cl}\) and one lone pair occupying the fourth \(sp^3\) orbital in a trigonal pyramidal geometry, as illustrated in the figure on the left.</p>

<p>Like \(\ce{C}\), when \(\ce{N}\) has one double bond, it is \(sp^2\) hybridized and when it ha triple bond it is \(sp\) hybridized. \(\ce{H2C=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{N}H}\) is an example of \(sp^2\) hybridized \(\ce{N}\) with the lone pair in an \(sp^2\) orbital and  \(\ce{CH3-C\equiv N\!:}\) is an example of \(sp\) hybridized \(\ce{N}\) in organic compound with a lone pair in an \(sp\) orbital. </p>

<p><img alt="sp3 hybridized oxgyen in water" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487040/clipboard_edf10e158e59a391e2d3509e56aa60d7a.png?revision=1" /><img alt="sp2 geometry of oxygen in formadehyde" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487043/clipboard_eb12119dd1b9a2bc7cebcc822abc01f7c.png?revision=1" />\(\ce{O}\) has six valence electrons. When \(\ce{O}\) has all single bonds, it is \(sp^3\) hybridized with two \(\sigma\) bonds and two lone pairs in \(sp^3\) orbitals. For example, \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{H2\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!:}\) is \(sp^3\) hybridized with two \(\sigma_{s-sp^3}\) orbitals and two lone pairs in \(sp^3\) orbitals in a bent molecular geometry, as illustrated in Figure on the left. \(\ce{CH3-\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}}-H}\) is and example of \(sp^3\) hybridized \(\ce{O}\) in an organic compound. When there is one double bond on \(\ce{O}\) it is \(sp^2\) hybridized with two \(\sigma\) bonds, a \(\pi\) bond, and two lone pairs in \(sp^2\) orbitals, as in \(\ce{CH2=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}}}\), illustrated in the figure on the right. \(\ce{S}\) is in the same group with \(\ce{O}\) and its compounds can have similar hybridiztion as \(\ce{O}\). For example, \(\ce{S}\) is \(sp^3\) hybridized in \(\ce{CH3-\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{S}}-H}\) and \(sp^2\) hybridized in \(\ce{CH2=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{S}}}\) similar to oxygen compounds.</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Quickly_recognizing_the_hybridization_and_geometries_of_atoms_in_molecules"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Quickly recognizing the hybridization and geometries of atoms in molecules</span></h5>

<ul>
    <li><span style="font-size:17.6px;">When \(\ce{C}\), \(\ce{N}\), and \(\ce{O}\) have octet complements in molecules with only single bonds, they are \(sp^3\) hybridized, e.g., \(\ce{C}\) in </span>\(\ce{CH4}\), \(\ce{N}\) in \(\ce{H3N\!:}\), and \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{H2\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!:}\) is \(sp^3\).

    <ul>
        <li><span style="font-size:17.6px;">When they have one \(\pi\) bond (a double bond), they are \(sp^2\) hybridized, e.g., \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{O}|) in </span>\(\ce{CH2=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}}}\)<span style="font-size:17.6px;">, and \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{N}\) in </span>\(\ce{H2C=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{N}H}\).

        <ul>
            <li><span style="font-size:17.6px;">When they have two \(\pi\) bonds (a triple bond or two double bonds), they are \(sp\) hybridized, e.g. \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{O=C=O}\) and \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{N}\) in </span>\(\ce{CH3-C\equiv N\!:}\)</li>
        </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>When a \(sp^3\) hybridized atom has no lone pair, it has a tetrahedral molecular geometry, e.g. \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{CH4}\).
    <ul>
        <li>When there is one lone pair, it has trigonal pyramidal geometry, e.g., \(\ce{N}\) in  \(\ce{H3N\!:}\)
        <ul>
            <li>When it has two lone pairs, it has bent geometry, e.g., \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{H2\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}\!:}\) is \(sp^3\).</li>
        </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>When an \(sp^2\) hybridized atom has no lone pair, it has a triangular planar geometry, e.g, \(\ce{C}\) in \(\ce{CH2=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}}}\).
    <ul>
        <li>When there is one lone pair, it has bent geometry, e.g., <span style="font-size:17.6px;">\(\ce{N}\) in </span>\(\ce{H2C=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{N}H}\).
        <ul>
            <li>When it has two lone pairs, it has linear geometry, e.g., \(\ce{O}\) in \(\ce{CH2=\overset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{\underset{\Large\cdot\cdot}{O}}}\).</li>
        </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
    </li>
    <li>\(sp\) hybridized atoms always have linear geometry around them, e.g, \(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{N}\) in <span style="font-size:17.6px;">\(\ce{C}\) and \(\ce{N}\) in </span>\(\ce{CH3-C\equiv N\!:}\)</li>
</ul>

<p>Structures of these examples are illustrated in this and the previous section.</p>
</div></section>

</div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_13"><span id="Reactive_intermediates"></span><h3 class="editable">Reactive intermediates</h3>

<p>In addition to the stable form \(\ce{C}\) commonly found in organic compounds that has four covalent bonds and may be \(sp^3\), \(sp^2\), or \(sp\)-hybridized, several reactive intermediates are high energy reactive and short-lived species, formed during chemical reactions and quickly convert into more stable species. Geometry and hybridization of some of these are described here.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_14"><span id="Carbocation"></span><h4 class="editable">Carbocation</h4>

<p><img alt="sp3-hybrid structure of a carbocation" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488366/clipboard_eca4b39970021441174a2cbd64c5f357c.png?revision=1" />Carbocation, more specifically, carbenium ion, is an \(sp^2\)-hybridized \(\ce{C}\) with +1 formal charge, three covalent bonds and an empty \(p\)-orbital, as illustrated on the right. Its exampels inlcude tertiary butyl cation  \(\ce{(CH3)3\overset{+}{C}}\), triphenylmethyl cation \(\ce{(C6H5)3\overset{+}{C}}\), etc. Its structure is trigonal planar with an empty \(p\) orbital perpendicular to the trigonal plane. The \(\ce{C}\) in a carbenium ion has six valence electrons, i.e., octet incomplete. It is highly reactive and tends to complete its octet during chemical reactions.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_15"><span id="Carbanion"></span><h4 class="editable">Carbanion</h4>

<p><img alt="tetrahederal geometry of a carbanion" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488367/clipboard_e8052e8331060fd2965c007431acc4daf.png?revision=1" />A carbanion is an anion with a lone pair on a tetravalent carbon, as illustrated in the figure on the left. Its examples include  ethanide \(\ce{CH3\overset{-}{C}H2}\), <i>t</i>-butanide \(\ce{(CH3)3\overset{-}{C}}\), triphenylmethanide \(\ce{(C6H5)3\overset{-}{C}}\), etc. It is usually \(sp^3\)-hybridized with lone pair in one \(sp^3\)-orbital, resulting in a trigonal pyramidal geometry,  like that of ammonia. It has eight valence electrons, is still reactive because of the negative charge, and tends to react and convert into neutral, more stable species in chemical reactions.</p>
</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_16"><span id="Carbon_radical"></span><h4 class="editable">Carbon radical</h4>

<p><img alt="trigonal planar carbon radical" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488368/clipboard_e22ed59b7b83c23a74187c0506e38ddfe.png?revision=1" /><img alt="Pyramidal structure of a carbon radical" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488369/clipboard_e288c133ba6ea4b25a9af20339cf3a03a.png?revision=1" />Carbon radical is a molecule containing a \(\ce{C}\) with zero formal charge and an unpaired electron. Its examples include methyl radical \(\ce{\overset{\Huge\cdot}{C}H3}\), <i>t</i>-butyl radical \(\ce{(CH3)3\overset{\Huge \cdot}{C}}\), triphenylmethyl radical \(\ce{(C6H5)3\overset{\Huge \cdot}{C}}\), etc. It is usually \(sp^2\) hybridized with an unpaired electron in \(p\)-orbital, as illustrated in the figure on the right. Still, pyramidal geometries are also observed in some cases, as illustrated in the figure on the left. Since the radical \(\ce{C}\) has an incomplete octet with seven valence electrons, it is highly reactive and tends to make covalent bonds to complete its octet in chemical reactions.</p>
</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_17"><span id="Carbene"></span><h4 class="editable">Carbene</h4>

<p><img alt="sp2 structure of a singlet carbene" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488370/clipboard_eac695c812286a563c8529a9d2df6c7ed.png?revision=1" /><img alt="tetrahederal geometry of triplet carbene" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/488371/clipboard_e855d6b0bcd0eceab596912796581b204.png?revision=1" />Carbene is a molecule containing a neutral \(\ce{C}\) with two covalent bonds and two non-bonding electrons. Its examples include dichlorocarbene \(\ce{Cl2C\!\!\Large :}\) and methylene \(\ce{H2C\!\!\Large :}\). There are two classes of carbene, singlet and triplet. A singlet carbene is usually \(sp^2\)-hybridized \(\ce{C}\) with trigonal planer geometry, containing two covalent bonds and a lone pair in an \(sp^2\)-orbital and an empty \(p\)-orbital perpendicular to the trigonal plane, as illustrated in the figure on the left. A triplet carbene is usually \(sp^3\)-hybridized \(\ce{C}\) with two covalent bonds and two \(sp^3\)-orbitals occupied by an unpaired electron, as illustrated in the figure on the right. Since it has an octet incomplete with six valence electrons and empty or singly occupied valence orbitals, it is a highly reactive intermediate.</p>
</div></div></div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_18"><span id="Molecular_orbital_(MO)_theory"></span><h2 class="editable">Molecular orbital (MO) theory</h2>

<p><img alt="wave superpostion illustrated" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487053/clipboard_edf7834e2dd7b2b8ce08629c3bc8485f7.png?revision=1" />Atomic orbitals (AO) are regions around the nucleus where electrons are most likely found. AOs are obtained by wave functions treating electrons as waves. Interference of two or more waves (wave superposition) can be constructive interference, enhancing each other, or destructive interference, cancelling each other, as illustrated in the figure on the right (Copyright: Public domain). Similarly, molecular orbital (MO) theory describes molecular orbitals (MOs) derived mathematically by a linear combination of atomic orbitals of compatible symmetry, creating an equal number of molecular orbitals. For example, an electron wavefunction of one \(\ce{H}\), \(\psi_1\) may combine with that of second \(\ce{H}\), \(\psi_2\), either constructively resulting in a bonding MO (\(\Psi = \psi_{2} + \psi_{2}\)) or destructively resulting in antibonding MO (\(\Psi^{*} = \psi_{2} + \psi_{2}\)).</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Types_of_molecular_orbitals"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Types of molecular orbitals</span></h5>

<p>The \(\Psi\) is called a <strong>bonding molecular orbital</strong> because it places electrons more between the nuclei in the overlapped region, resulting in an increased attractive force and lowering of potential energy than the AOs. The \(\Psi^*\) is an <strong>antibonding molecular orbital</strong> because it cancels the orbital between the nuclei, resulting in higher potential energy than AOs, as illustrated in Figure 1.4.7. If an atomic orbital has no compatible orbital to interact with, it becomes a <strong>nonbonding molecular orbital</strong> of energy equal to the corresponding atomic orbital.</p>
</div></section>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e46997069a8f2f3c4c0353e30b478487f.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487056/clipboard_e46997069a8f2f3c4c0353e30b478487f.png?revision=1" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{7}\): Illustration of molecular orbital (MO) constructive and destructive interference of \(\ce{H}\) atomic orbitals during \(\ce{H2}\) formation (right), resulting in bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals (middle). The MO energy diagram is shown on the right. (Copyright; Public domain)</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Filling_molecular_orbitals_with_electrons"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Filling molecular orbitals with electrons</span></h5>

<p>Molecular orbitals are usually shown in an energy diagram to show their relative energies, as illustrated in Figure 1.4.7. In the energy diagram, the AOs are drawn on the sides, and the MOs are in the middle. The MOs are filled with the available valence electrons of the bonded atoms by the same rules as for AOs, i.e., i) the lowest energy orbital is filled first, each orbital can take a maximum of two electrons provided they have opposite spins, and iii) degenerate orbitals are filled singly until all of them is singly occupied, before electron pairing begins. </p>
</div></section>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Naming_molecular_orbitals"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Naming molecular orbitals</span></h5>

<p><img alt="illustration of sigma p-p molecuilar orbitals" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487058/clipboard_e9ce98814aba5e7b790b2e5ed2680bcbc.png?revision=1" /><img alt="illustration of pi pp orbitals" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487060/clipboard_e9cd5513f868ebf8c0b85c6dafd1268c2.png?revision=1" />The MOs are named \(\sigma\), \(\pi\), \(\delta\), etc. A \(\sigma\) orbital is formed by the head-on overlap of atomic orbitals, resulting in a cylindrically symmetrical electron density distribution on the bond axis. The bonding and antibonding orbitals formed by a combination of \(1s\) AOs of \(\ce{H}\), shown in Figure 1.4.7, are an example of \(\sigma\) MO. Figure on the right illustrates \(\sigma\) orbitals formed by overlaping \(p\) orbitals.</p>

<p>A \(\pi\) MO is formed by a lateral overlap of atomic orbitals, e.g., two parallel \(p\) orbitals, placing electron density above and below the bond axis with a nodal plane passing through the bond axis. Figure on the left illustrates \(\pi\) bonds formed by overlapping \(p\) orbitals. \(\delta\) and other MOs are not described here.</p>
</div></section>

<p><img alt="MO diagram of F2" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487063/clipboard_e68752adfc91b54a0a2c935c090fd82bd.png?revision=1" /><img alt="MO diagram of O2" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487064/clipboard_e0f9d514d8bc5160c8e2b51e464054468.png?revision=1" />A linear combination of \(n\) atomic orbitals produces \(n\) molecular orbitals by the following general formula \(\sum_{n=1}^{n} c_{n}\psi_{n} = \Psi_{n}\), where \(\Psi_{n}\) is the n<sup>th </sup>molecular orbital, \(\psi\) are atomic orbitals, and \(c_n\) are weighted contribution of the atomic orbitals ranging form 1 to \(n\).</p>

<p>Figure on the right illustratres MO energy diagram of \(\ce{HF}\) molecule, where \(1\pi\) are nonbonding molecular orbitals derived from \(2p_x\) and \(2p_y\) atomic orbitals of \(\ce{F}\) without any interference. Figure on the left illustrates the MO energy diagram of \(\ce{O2}\). Note that it shows two unpaired electrons in the degenerate \(\pi^*\) molecular orbitals. The unpaired electrons in \(\ce{O2}\) explain its paramagnetic property of \(\ce{O2}\) explain its paramagnetic property that valence bond theory could not explain. </p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Highest_occupied_molecular_orbital_(HOME)_and_lowest_unoccupied_molecular_orbitals_(LUMO)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Highest occupied molecular orbital (HOME) and lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals (LUMO)</span></h5>

<p>Two of the molecular orbitals are important in describing their chemical reactions. One is the highest energy occupied molecular orbital (HOMO), e.g., \(\pi^*\) in the case of \(\ce{O2}\) and \(1\pi\) in the case of \(\ce{HF}\) shown in the energy diagrams on the left and right. The other is the lowest energy unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO), e.g., \(\sigma^*\) in the case of \(\ce{O2}\) and \(4\pi\) in the case of \(\ce{HF}\) shown in the energy diagrams on the left and right.</p>
</div></section>

<p><img alt="A bonding MO of CH3Br" class="internal right" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487066/clipboard_e3f4174d0f35bc54bb85cbacdf484986b.png?revision=1" /><img alt="LUMO of CH3Br" class="internal left" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487067/clipboard_eea68c84035ebf901482b7baf128e4567.png?revision=1" />Atomic orbitals are associated with atoms. Valence bond theory describes primarily the orbitals localized between two bonded atoms. Molecular orbital theory describes molecular orbitals related to molecules. Some of the molecular orbitals may span the entire molecule. For example, the figure on the right shows one of the bonding molecular orbitals of \(\ce{CH3Br}\) that spans the whole molecule. The figure on the left shows the LUMO of \(\ce{CH3Br}\), showing phases of the wavefunction in red and blue colors. It will be described in later chapters concerning the reaction of \(\ce{CH3Br}\).</p>

<p>Most of the time, the valence bond theory and the molecular orbital theory explain the chemical phenomena equally well. Valence bond theory will be used more often because it is relatively simple. However, molecular orbital theory sometimes offers a better explanation and is needed. Therefore, a hybrid approach combining the two theories will be employed in the later chapters to explain the chemical concepts.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section" id="section_19"><span id="Bond_strength"></span><h2 class="editable">Bond strength</h2>

<p>One measure of bond strength is the bond dissociation energy (BDE)</p>

<section class="box-definition">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Organic_Chemistry/Organic_Chemistry_-Part_1_Fundamentals_(Malik)/01:_Bonding_and_structure/1.04:_Bonding_theories_and_geometries_of_molecules" class="mt-section"><span id="Bond_dissociation_energy_(BDE)"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">Bond dissociation energy (BDE)</span></h5>

<p>Bond dissociation energy is the amount of energy released as heat when \(\ce{A-B}\) cleaves into fragments \(\ce{A^\cdot}\) and \(\ce{B^\cdot}\). </p>
</div></section>

<p>The following are some of the factors affecting bond strength.</p>

<ul>
    <li>A bond between smaller valence orbitals is usually stronger than between larger orbitals because of the larger distance between the shared electrons and the nuclei.</li>
    <li>Other factors being equal, a triple bond is stronger and shorter than a double bond, and a double bond is stronger and shorter than a single bond. This is because the number of shared electrons is six in a triple bond, four in a double bond, and two in a single bond.</li>
    <li>A \(\sigma\) bond formed by head-on overlap is stronger than a \(\pi\) bond formed by lateral overlap due to more overlap in the former case. </li>
    <li>A \(\pi\) bond is strongest when the overlapping orbitals are parallel, becomes weaker with less overlap as the orbitals rotate from parallel orientation, and becomes zero when they are at 90&deg; from each other, due to the overlap of + lobe being equal with the + and the -lobes of the other cancelling each other and voice versa,  as illustrated in Figure 1.4.8 a) and b).</li>
    <li>A bond between two orbitals of comparable sizes is stronger than when the overlapping orbitals are of different sizes; the larger the difference in sizes, the weaker the overlap and the weaker the bond. Comparison of Figure 1.4.8 a) and c) illustrates this point.</li>
    <li>A \(\sigma\) bond is strongest when the overlapping orbitals are oriented parallel along the bond-axis and become weaker when they are bent from the parallel orientation. Comparison of Figure 1.4.8 d) and e) illustrates this point.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img alt="clipboard_e7097539b7afdd6dc465b93f60fde1b5a.png" class="internal" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/487106/clipboard_e7097539b7afdd6dc465b93f60fde1b5a.png?revision=1" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{8}\): Illustration of two orbitals making a strong \(\pi\) bond when they are of comparable sizes in parallel orientation (a) becoming weaker and reaching zero bond when they rotate to 90 &deg; (b), become weaker with less overlap when they are of different sizes (c). Copy and Paste Caption here. (Copyright; Public domain)</figcaption>
</figure>

